Author: Steven Rostedt <srostedt-H+wXaHxf7aLQT0dZR+AlfA@public.gmane.org>
Date:   Thu Jun 18 12:45:08 2009 -0400

    function-graph: add stack frame test
   =20
    In case gcc does something funny with the stack frames, or the return
    from function code, we would like to detect that.
   =20
    An arch may implement passing of a variable that is unique to the
    function and can be saved on entering a function and can be tested
    when exiting the function. Usually the frame pointer can be used for
    this purpose.
   =20
    This patch also implements this for x86. Where it passes in the stack
    frame of the parent function, and will test that frame on exit.
   =20
    There was a case in x86_32 with optimize for size (-Os) where, for a
    few functions, gcc would align the stack frame and place a copy of th=
e
    return address into it. The function graph tracer modified the copy a=
nd
    not the actual return address. On return from the funtion, it did not=
 go
    to the tracer hook, but returned to the parent. This broke the functi=
on
    graph tracer, because the return of the parent (where gcc did not do
    this funky manipulation) returned to the location that the child func=
tion
    was suppose to. This caused strange kernel crashes.
   =20
    This test detected the problem and pointed out where the issue was.
   =20
    This modifies the parameters of one of the functions that the arch
    specific code calls, so it includes changes to arch code to accommoda=
te
    the new prototype.
   =20
    Note, I notice that the parsic arch implements its own push_return_tr=
ace.
    This is now a generic function and the ftrace_push_return_trace shoul=
d be
    used instead. This patch does not touch that code.
   =20
    Cc: Benjamin Herrenschmidt <benh-XVmvHMARGAS8U2dJNN8I7kB+6BGkLq7r@public.gmane.org>
    Cc: Paul Mackerras <paulus-eUNUBHrolfbYtjvyW6yDsg@public.gmane.org>
