Author: Jan Kara <jack-AlSwsSmVLrQ@public.gmane.org>
Date:   Wed Jun 17 16:26:20 2009 -0700

    ext2: Do not update mtime of a moved directory
   =20
    One of our users is complaining that his backup tool is upset on ext2
    (while it's happy on ext3, xfs, ...) because of the mtime change.
   =20
    The problem is:
   =20
        mkdir foo
        mkdir bar
        mkdir foo/a
   =20
    Now under ext2:
        mv foo/a foo/b
   =20
    changes mtime of 'foo/a' (foo/b after the move).  That does not reall=
y
    make sense and it does not happen under any other filesystem I've see=
n.
   =20
    More complicated is:
        mv foo/a bar/a
   =20
    This changes mtime of foo/a (bar/a after the move) and it makes some
    sense since we had to update parent directory pointer of foo/a.  But
    again, no other filesystem does this.  So after some thoughts I'd vot=
e
    for consistency and change ext2 to behave the same as other filesyste=
ms.
   =20
    Do not update mtime of a moved directory.  Specs don't say anything
    about it (neither that it should, nor that it should not be updated) =
and
    other common filesystems (ext3, ext4, xfs, reiserfs, fat, ...) don't =
do
    it.  So let's become more consistent.
   =20
    Spotted by ronny.pretzsch-1hiIy/nQ5+o@public.gmane.org, initial fix by J=C3=B6rn Engel.
   =20
