Author: Wu Fengguang <fengguang.wu-ral2JQCrhuEAvxtiuMwx3w@public.gmane.org>
Date:   Tue Jun 16 15:33:17 2009 -0700

    writeback: skip new or to-be-freed inodes
   =20
    1) I_FREEING tests should be coupled with I_CLEAR
   =20
    The two I_FREEING tests are racy because clear_inode() can set i_stat=
e to
    I_CLEAR between the clear of I_SYNC and the test of I_FREEING.
   =20
    2) skip I_WILL_FREE inodes in generic_sync_sb_inodes() to avoid possi=
ble
       races with generic_forget_inode()
   =20
    generic_forget_inode() sets I_WILL_FREE call writeback on its own, so
    generic_sync_sb_inodes() shall not try to step in and create possible=
 races:
   =20
      generic_forget_inode
        inode->i_state |=3D I_WILL_FREE;
        spin_unlock(&inode_lock);
                                           generic_sync_sb_inodes()
                                             spin_lock(&inode_lock);
                                             __iget(inode);
                                             __writeback_single_inode
                                               // see non zero i_count
     may WARN here =3D=3D>                         WARN_ON(inode->i_state=
 & I_WILL_FREE);
                                             spin_unlock(&inode_lock);
     may call generic_forget_inode again =3D=3D> iput(inode);
   =20
    The above race and warning didn't turn up because writeback_inodes() =
holds
    the s_umount lock, so generic_forget_inode() finds MS_ACTIVE and retu=
rns
    early.  But we are not sure the UBIFS calls and future callers will
    guarantee that.  So skip I_WILL_FREE inodes for the sake of safety.
   =20
