Author: Wu Fengguang <fengguang.wu-ral2JQCrhuEAvxtiuMwx3w@public.gmane.org>
Date:   Tue Jun 16 15:31:28 2009 -0700

    readahead: sequential mmap readahead
   =20
    Auto-detect sequential mmap reads and do readahead for them.
   =20
    The sequential mmap readahead will be triggered when
    - sync readahead: it's a major fault and (prev_offset =3D=3D offset-1=
);
    - async readahead: minor fault on PG_readahead page with valid readah=
ead state.
   =20
    The benefits of doing readahead instead of read-around:
    - less I/O wait thanks to async readahead
    - double real I/O size and no more cache hits
   =20
    The single stream case is improved a little.
    For 100,000 sequential mmap reads:
   =20
                                        user       system    cpu        t=
otal
    (1-1)  plain -mm, 128KB readaround: 3.224      2.554     48.40%     1=
1.838
    (1-2)  plain -mm, 256KB readaround: 3.170      2.392     46.20%     1=
1.976
    (2)  patched -mm, 128KB readahead:  3.117      2.448     47.33%     1=
1.607
   =20
    The patched (2) has smallest total time, since it has no cache hit ov=
erheads
    and less I/O block time(thanks to async readahead). Here the I/O size
    makes no much difference, since there's only one single stream.
   =20
    Note that (1-1)'s real I/O size is 64KB and (1-2)'s real I/O size is =
128KB,
    since the half of the read-around pages will be readahead cache hits.
   =20
    This is going to make _real_ differences for _concurrent_ IO streams.
   =20
