Author: Sarah Sharp <sarah.a.sharp-VuQAYsv1563Yd54FQh9/CA@public.gmane.org>
Date:   Thu May 14 11:44:18 2009 -0700

    USB: xhci: Remove packed attribute from structures.
   =20
    The packed attribute allows gcc to muck with the alignment of data
    structures, which may lead to byte-wise writes that break atomicity o=
f
    writes.  Packed should only be used when the compile may add undesire=
d
    padding to the structure.  Each element of the structure will be alig=
ned
    by C based on its size and the size of the elements around it.  E.g. =
a u64
    would be aligned on an 8 byte boundary, the next u32 would be aligned=
 on a
    four byte boundary, etc.
   =20
    Since most of the xHCI structures contain only u32 bit values, removi=
ng
    the packed attribute for them should be harmless.  (A future patch wi=
ll
    change some of the twin 32-bit address fields to one 64-bit field, bu=
t all
    those places have an even number of 32-bit fields before them, so the
    alignment should be correct.)  Add BUILD_BUG_ON statements to check t=
hat
    the compiler doesn't add padding to the data structures that have a
    hardware-defined layout.
   =20
    While we're modifying the registers, change the name of intr_reg to
    xhci_intr_reg to avoid global conflicts.
   =20
    Signed-off-by: Sarah Sharp <sarah.a.sharp-VuQAYsv1563Yd54FQh9/CA@public.gmane.org>
    Signed-off-by: Greg Kroah-Hartman <gregkh-l3A5Bk7waGM@public.gmane.org>

