Author: Sarah Sharp <sarah.a.sharp-VuQAYsv1563Yd54FQh9/CA@public.gmane.org>
Date:   Mon Apr 27 19:52:34 2009 -0700

    USB: xhci: Ring allocation and initialization.
   =20
    Allocate basic xHCI host controller data structures.  For every xHC, =
there
    is a command ring, an event ring, and a doorbell array.
   =20
    The doorbell array is used to notify the host controller that work ha=
s
    been enqueued onto one of the rings.  The host controller driver enqu=
eues
    commands on the command ring.  The HW enqueues command completion eve=
nts
    on the event ring and interrupts the system (currently using PCI
    interrupts, although the xHCI HW will use MSI interrupts eventually).
   =20
    All rings and the doorbell array must be allocated by the xHCI host
    controller driver.
   =20
    Each ring is comprised of one or more segments, which consists of 16-=
byte
    Transfer Request Blocks (TRBs) that can be chained to form a Transfer
    Descriptor (TD) that represents a multiple-buffer request.  Segments =
are
    linked into a ring using Link TRBs, which means they are dynamically
    growable.
   =20
    The producer of the ring enqueues a TD by writing one or more TRBs in=
 the
    ring and toggling the TRB cycle bit for each TRB.  The consumer knows=
 it
    can process the TRB when the cycle bit matches its internal consumer =
cycle
    state for the ring.  The consumer cycle state is toggled an odd amoun=
t of
    times in the ring.
   =20
    An example ring (a ring must have a minimum of 16 TRBs on it, but tha=
t's
    too big to draw in ASCII art):
   =20
                  chain  cycle
                   bit    bit
     ------------------------
    | TD A TRB 1 |  1  |  1  |<-------------  <-- consumer dequeue ptr
     ------------------------               |     consumer cycle state =3D=
 1
    | TD A TRB 2 |  1  |  1  |              |
     ------------------------               |
    | TD A TRB 3 |  0  |  1  |  segment 1   |
     ------------------------               |
    | TD B TRB 1 |  1  |  1  |              |
     ------------------------               |
    | TD B TRB 2 |  0  |  1  |              |
     ------------------------               |
    | Link TRB   |  0  |  1  |-----         |
     ------------------------     |         |
                                  |         |
                  chain  cycle    |         |
                   bit    bit     |         |
     ------------------------     |         |
    | TD C TRB 1 |  0  |  1  |<----         |
     ------------------------               |
    | TD D TRB 1 |  1  |  1  |              |
     ------------------------               |
    | TD D TRB 2 |  1  |  1  |   segment 2  |
     ------------------------               |
    | TD D TRB 3 |  1  |  1  |              |
     ------------------------               |
    | TD D TRB 4 |  1  |  1  |              |
     ------------------------               |
    | Link TRB   |  1  |  1  |-----         |
     ------------------------     |         |
                                  |         |
                  chain  cycle    |         |
                   bit    bit     |         |
     ------------------------     |         |
    | TD D TRB 5 |  1  |  1  |<----         |
     ------------------------               |
    | TD D TRB 6 |  0  |  1  |              |
     ------------------------               |
    | TD E TRB 1 |  0  |  1  |   segment 3  |
     ------------------------               |
    |            |  0  |  0  |              | <-- producer enqueue ptr
     ------------------------               |
    |            |  0  |  0  |              |
     ------------------------               |
    | Link TRB   |  0  |  0  |---------------
     ------------------------
   =20
    Signed-off-by: Sarah Sharp <sarah.a.sharp-VuQAYsv1563Yd54FQh9/CA@public.gmane.org>
    Signed-off-by: Greg Kroah-Hartman <gregkh-l3A5Bk7waGM@public.gmane.org>

