Author: Li Zefan <lizf-BthXqXjhjHXQFUHtdCDX3A@public.gmane.org>
Date:   Tue Jun 9 13:43:05 2009 +0800

    tracing/events: convert block trace points to TRACE_EVENT()
   =20
    TRACE_EVENT is a more generic way to define tracepoints. Doing so add=
s
    these new capabilities to this tracepoint:
   =20
      - zero-copy and per-cpu splice() tracing
      - binary tracing without printf overhead
      - structured logging records exposed under /debug/tracing/events
      - trace events embedded in function tracer output and other plugins
      - user-defined, per tracepoint filter expressions
      ...
   =20
    Cons:
   =20
      - no dev_t info for the output of plug, unplug_timer and unplug_io =
events.
        no dev_t info for getrq and sleeprq events if bio =3D=3D NULL.
        no dev_t info for rq_abort,...,rq_requeue events if rq->rq_disk =3D=
=3D NULL.
   =20
        This is mainly because we can't get the deivce from a request que=
ue.
        But this may change in the future.
   =20
      - A packet command is converted to a string in TP_assign, not TP_pr=
int.
        While blktrace do the convertion just before output.
   =20
        Since pc requests should be rather rare, this is not a big issue.
   =20
      - In blktrace, an event can have 2 different print formats, but a T=
RACE_EVENT
        has a unique format, which means we have some unused data in a tr=
ace entry.
   =20
        The overhead is minimized by using __dynamic_array() instead of _=
_array().
   =20
    I've benchmarked the ioctl blktrace vs the splice based TRACE_EVENT t=
racing:
   =20
          dd                   dd + ioctl blktrace       dd + TRACE_EVENT=
 (splice)
    1     7.36s, 42.7 MB/s     7.50s, 42.0 MB/s          7.41s, 42.5 MB/s
    2     7.43s, 42.3 MB/s     7.48s, 42.1 MB/s          7.43s, 42.4 MB/s
    3     7.38s, 42.6 MB/s     7.45s, 42.2 MB/s          7.41s, 42.5 MB/s
   =20
    So the overhead of tracing is very small, and no regression when usin=
g
    those trace events vs blktrace.
   =20
    And the binary output of TRACE_EVENT is much smaller than blktrace:
   =20
     # ls -l -h
     -rw-r--r-- 1 root root 8.8M 06-09 13:24 sda.blktrace.0
     -rw-r--r-- 1 root root 195K 06-09 13:24 sda.blktrace.1
     -rw-r--r-- 1 root root 2.7M 06-09 13:25 trace_splice.out
   =20
    Following are some comparisons between TRACE_EVENT and blktrace:
   =20
    plug:
      kjournald-480   [000]   303.084981: block_plug: [kjournald]
      kjournald-480   [000]   303.084981:   8,0    P   N [kjournald]
   =20
    unplug_io:
      kblockd/0-118   [000]   300.052973: block_unplug_io: [kblockd/0] 1
      kblockd/0-118   [000]   300.052974:   8,0    U   N [kblockd/0] 1
   =20
    remap:
      kjournald-480   [000]   303.085042: block_remap: 8,0 W 102736992 + =
8 <- (8,8) 33384
      kjournald-480   [000]   303.085043:   8,0    A   W 102736992 + 8 <-=
 (8,8) 33384
   =20
    bio_backmerge:
      kjournald-480   [000]   303.085086: block_bio_backmerge: 8,0 W 1027=
37032 + 8 [kjournald]
      kjournald-480   [000]   303.085086:   8,0    M   W 102737032 + 8 [k=
journald]
   =20
    getrq:
      kjournald-480   [000]   303.084974: block_getrq: 8,0 W 102736984 + =
8 [kjournald]
      kjournald-480   [000]   303.084975:   8,0    G   W 102736984 + 8 [k=
journald]
   =20
      bash-2066  [001]  1072.953770:   8,0    G   N [bash]
      bash-2066  [001]  1072.953773: block_getrq: 0,0 N 0 + 0 [bash]
   =20
    rq_complete:
