    block: Export I/O topology for block devices and partitions
   =20
    To support devices with physical block sizes bigger than 512 bytes we
    need to ensure proper alignment.  This patch adds support for exposin=
g
    I/O topology characteristics as devices are stacked.
   =20
      logical_block_size is the smallest unit the device can address.
   =20
      physical_block_size indicates the smallest I/O the device can write
      without incurring a read-modify-write penalty.
   =20
      The io_min parameter is the smallest preferred I/O size reported by
      the device.  In many cases this is the same as the physical block
      size.  However, the io_min parameter can be scaled up when stacking
      (RAID5 chunk size > physical block size).
   =20
      The io_opt characteristic indicates the optimal I/O size reported b=
y
      the device.  This is usually the stripe width for arrays.
   =20
      The alignment_offset parameter indicates the number of bytes the st=
art
      of the device/partition is offset from the device's natural alignme=
nt.
      Partition tools and MD/DM utilities can use this to pad their offse=
ts
      so filesystems start on proper boundaries.
   =20
    Signed-off-by: Martin K. Petersen <martin.petersen-QHcLZuEGTsvQT0dZR+AlfA@public.gmane.org>
    Signed-off-by: Jens Axboe <jens.axboe-QHcLZuEGTsvQT0dZR+AlfA@public.gmane.org>

