Author: Eric Dumazet <dada1-fPLkHRcR87vqlBn2x/YWAg@public.gmane.org>
Date:   Tue May 12 20:48:02 2009 +0000

    bnx2: bnx2_tx_int() optimizations
   =20
    When using bnx2 in a high transmit load, bnx2_tx_int() cost is pretty=
 high.
   =20
    There are two reasons.
   =20
    One is an expensive call to bnx2_get_hw_tx_cons(bnapi) for each freed=
 skb
   =20
    One is cpu stalls when accessing skb_is_gso(skb) / skb_shinfo(skb)->n=
r_frags
    because of two cache line misses.
    (One to get skb->end/head to compute skb_shinfo(skb),
     one to get is_gso/nr_frags)
   =20
    This patch :
   =20
    1) avoids calling bnx2_get_hw_tx_cons(bnapi) too many times.
   =20
    2) makes bnx2_start_xmit() cache is_gso & nr_frags into sw_tx_bd desc=
riptor.
       This uses a litle bit more ram (256 longs per device on x86), but =
helps a lot.
   =20
    3) uses a prefetch(&skb->end) to speedup dev_kfree_skb(), bringing
      cache line that will be needed in skb_release_data()
   =20
    result is 5 % bandwidth increase in benchmarks, involving UDP or TCP =
receive
     & transmits, when a cpu is dedicated to ksoftirqd for bnx2.
   =20
    bnx2_tx_int going from 3.33 % cpu to 0.5 % cpu in oprofile
   =20
    Note : skb_dma_unmap() still very expensive but this is for another p=
atch,
    not related to bnx2 (2.9 % of cpu, while it does nothing on x86_32)
   =20
    Signed-off-by: Eric Dumazet <dada1-fPLkHRcR87vqlBn2x/YWAg@public.gmane.org>
    Signed-off-by: David S. Miller <davem-fT/PcQaiUtIeIZ0/mPfg9Q@public.gmane.org>

