Author: Steven Rostedt <srostedt-H+wXaHxf7aLQT0dZR+AlfA@public.gmane.org>
Date:   Tue May 5 21:16:11 2009 -0400

    ring-buffer: move big if statement down
   =20
    In the hot path of the ring buffer "__rb_reserve_next" there's a big
    if statement that does not even return back to the work flow.
   =20
    	code;
   =20
    	if (cross to next page) {
   =20
    		[ lots of code ]
   =20
    		return;
    	}
   =20
    	more code;
   =20
    The condition is even the unlikely path, although we do not denote it
    with an unlikely because gcc is fine with it. The condition is true w=
hen
    the write crosses a page boundary, and we need to start at a new page.
   =20
    Having this if statement makes it hard to read, but calling another
    function to do the work is also not appropriate, because we are using=
 a lot
    of variables that were set before the if statement, and we do not wan=
t to
    send them as parameters.
   =20
    This patch changes it to a goto:
   =20
    	code;
   =20
    	if (cross to next page)
    		goto next_page;
   =20
    	more code;
   =20
    	return;
   =20
    next_page:
   =20
    	[ lots of code]
   =20
    This makes the code easier to understand, and a bit more obvious.
   =20
    The output from gcc is practically identical. For some reason, gcc de=
cided
    to use different registers when I switched it to a goto. But other th=
an that,
    the logic is the same.
   =20
    [ Impact: easier to read code ]
   =20
    Signed-off-by: Steven Rostedt <rostedt-nx8X9YLhiw1AfugRpC6u6w@public.gmane.org>

