Author: Steven Rostedt <srostedt-H+wXaHxf7aLQT0dZR+AlfA@public.gmane.org>
Date:   Thu Apr 16 21:41:52 2009 -0400

    tracing: add same level recursion detection
   =20
    The tracing infrastructure allows for recursion. That is, an interrup=
t
    may interrupt the act of tracing an event, and that interrupt may ver=
y well
    perform its own trace. This is a recursive trace, and is fine to do.
   =20
    The problem arises when there is a bug, and the utility doing the tra=
ce
    calls something that recurses back into the tracer. This recursion is=
 not
    caused by an external event like an interrupt, but by code that is no=
t
    expected to recurse. The result could be a lockup.
   =20
    This patch adds a bitmask to the task structure that keeps track
    of the trace recursion. To find the interrupt depth, the following
    algorithm is used:
   =20
      level =3D hardirq_count() + softirq_count() + in_nmi;
   =20
    Here, level will be the depth of interrutps and softirqs, and even ha=
ndles
    the nmi. Then the corresponding bit is set in the recursion bitmask.
    If the bit was already set, we know we had a recursion at the same le=
vel
    and we warn about it and fail the writing to the buffer.
   =20
    After the data has been committed to the buffer, we clear the bit.
    No atomics are needed. The only races are with interrupts and they re=
set
    the bitmask before returning anywy.
   =20
    [ Impact: detect same irq level trace recursion ]
   =20
    Signed-off-by: Steven Rostedt <rostedt-nx8X9YLhiw1AfugRpC6u6w@public.gmane.org>

