    perfcounters: implement "counter inheritance"
   =20
    Impact: implement new performance feature
   =20
    Counter inheritance can be used to run performance counters in a work=
load,
    transparently - and pipe back the counter results to the parent count=
er.
   =20
    Inheritance for performance counters works the following way: when cr=
eating
    a counter it can be marked with the .inherit=3D1 flag. Such counters =
are then
    'inherited' by all child tasks (be they fork()-ed or clone()-ed). The=
se
    counters get inherited through exec() boundaries as well (except thro=
ugh
    setuid boundaries).
   =20
    The counter values get added back to the parent counter(s) when the c=
hild
    task(s) exit - much like stime/utime statistics are gathered. So inhe=
rited
    counters are ideal to gather summary statistics about an application'=
s
    behavior via shell commands, without having to modify that applicatio=
n.
   =20
    The timec.c command utilizes counter inheritance:
   =20
      http://redhat.com/~mingo/perfcounters/timec.c
   =20
    Sample output:
   =20
       $ ./timec -e 1 -e 3 -e 5 ls -lR /usr/include/ >/dev/null
   =20
       Performance counter stats for 'ls':
   =20
               163516953 instructions
                    2295 cache-misses
                 2855182 branch-misses
   =20
    Signed-off-by: Ingo Molnar <mingo-X9Un+BFzKDI@public.gmane.org>

